1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,840
It takes on almost any food you can imagine.

2
00:00:07,841 --> 00:00:10,240
It's a guilty pleasure.

3
00:00:10,241 --> 00:00:11,640
It's delicious.

4
00:00:11,641 --> 00:00:14,940
Hot crackling oil and careful timing.

5
00:00:14,941 --> 00:00:18,660
Foundries pouring the perfect pan, an industry devoted

6
00:00:18,661 --> 00:00:22,360
to special machines and secret recipes.

7
00:00:22,361 --> 00:00:24,200
Stronger looking good.

8
00:00:24,201 --> 00:00:26,460
It consumes billions of pounds of oil.

9
00:00:26,461 --> 00:00:27,460
If you don't make it hot enough,

10
00:00:27,461 --> 00:00:29,400
well, you'll have greasy turkey.

11
00:00:29,401 --> 00:00:31,100
And the more of it we devour,

12
00:00:31,101 --> 00:00:33,440
the farther this fire engine will travel.

13
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:36,780
My personal motto is, everything's better fried.

14
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:37,680
Unbelievable.

15
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:39,680
They're perfectly grounded

16
00:00:39,681 --> 00:00:41,980
and nice golden brown batter.

17
00:00:41,981 --> 00:00:45,480
Now, Fry It on Modern Marvels.

18
00:00:45,481 --> 00:00:58,960
Whether you toss it in or stir it up,

19
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,600
frying is the delicious union of sizzling hot oil

20
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:04,880
and bare or battered food.

21
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:08,200
It's not clear when we first began frying,

22
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:11,680
but we do know the ancient Egyptians

23
00:01:11,681 --> 00:01:15,080
feasted on fried fish over 4,000 years ago.

24
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,980
Now almost every culture loves it.

25
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:24,020
Every year, Americans alone consume more than 4 billion

26
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:28,520
pounds of French fries, 10 billion pounds of donuts,

27
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,420
and 24 billion pounds of fried chicken.

28
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:37,540
At the Los Angeles County Fair in Pomona, California,

29
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:39,540
thousands of visitors seek out the ultimate

30
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,040
in fried fair cuisine.

31
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,040
Sure, there's the hot, fresh funnel table.

32
00:01:45,041 --> 00:01:48,040
A funnel cake is a Pennsylvania Dutch dessert.

33
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:51,940
What makes this unique is it's light and fluffy on the inside

34
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,940
and it's crispy on the outside.

35
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:57,980
And there's the Mexican funnel cake, a churro with a twist.

36
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,880
Fried dough just seems to be the magical combination,

37
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,400
especially at the fair.

38
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,900
You throw a little sugar on top, then you really can't go wrong.

39
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Even mega-sized ones.

40
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,000
We didn't just make this for you guys this size.

41
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:11,900
This is the actual size.

42
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Feels about eight pounds right here.

43
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:15,240
right here to me.

44
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:18,600
So you could easily feed six to eight people,

45
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,500
but I've seen people try to take it on by themselves.

46
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,140
But for those who really want to get exotic,

47
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:28,540
the place to go is Chicken Charlie's.

48
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:30,520
It banks on the notion that almost everything

49
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:34,720
tastes better when it's fried, including frog's legs,

50
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:42,560
pop tarts, Spam, avocados, Oreos, and yes, even Twinkies.

51
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:47,080
I love to see the customer walking up to our place of business,

52
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,500
looking at the sign and saying, oh, my God, you're defrying what?

53
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:56,380
In order to keep things frying, Charlie heats over 600 gallons of soybean oil

54
00:02:56,381 --> 00:02:59,700
to roughly 375 degrees Fahrenheit each day.

55
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:04,660
You want to keep the oil clean, and you want to keep the right temperature,

56
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:06,920
but the biggest secret is the batter,

57
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,280
making sure what type of batter and the thickness of the batter.

58
00:03:10,281 --> 00:03:12,280
The batter not only affects flavor,

59
00:03:12,281 --> 00:03:14,280
but also acts as a barrier to the hot oil

60
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:17,280
penetrating the food beneath.

61
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:22,280
Charlie uses three basic batters for over 25 different items.

62
00:03:22,281 --> 00:03:26,280
A thick fish and chips batter is ideal for heavier foods,

63
00:03:26,281 --> 00:03:29,280
such as avocados, while sweeter items call

64
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:31,280
for something a little lighter.

65
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:33,280
This is basically like a pancake mix

66
00:03:33,281 --> 00:03:35,280
that's flour mixed with water.

67
00:03:35,281 --> 00:03:39,280
So what I use this for is to deep fry Oreos,

68
00:03:39,281 --> 00:03:42,980
Oreos, Pop-Tarts, strawberry Pop-Tarts.

69
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:45,280
You can fry many different types of fruits,

70
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:48,720
from bananas, strawberries, pineapples.

71
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:52,220
Fried Oreos may be one of Charlie's favorite creations,

72
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:54,420
and the Pop-Tarts his latest.

73
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:56,840
But it's the deep-fried Twinkie that started his frying

74
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:58,440
obsession.

75
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:00,740
I got some Twinkies over here I'm going to deep fry.

76
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,940
I'm going to use the egg wash and the sweet flour

77
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,540
and just toss it in there.

78
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:07,880
Nothing like a deep-fried Twinkie.

79
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:11,280
Now that we've fried them, we've got to put the toppings on them.

80
00:04:11,281 --> 00:04:13,680
We are going to go with strawberry and chocolate,

81
00:04:13,681 --> 00:04:15,120
so you still get the cream.

82
00:04:15,121 --> 00:04:16,720
You still taste the sponge.

83
00:04:16,721 --> 00:04:18,720
It still tastes like a Twinkie,

84
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:21,920
except with the crispiness on the outside.

85
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:25,160
Want to cope with that?

86
00:04:25,161 --> 00:04:27,440
As bizarre as it may sound,

87
00:04:27,441 --> 00:04:30,400
Charlie has even taken to frying soda.

88
00:04:30,401 --> 00:04:32,580
I'm going to pour it into this flour.

89
00:04:32,581 --> 00:04:34,280
I'm going to stir it up a little bit.

90
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:42,880
We're just gonna put it back in the cup we got it from.

91
00:04:42,881 --> 00:04:44,880
All right.

92
00:04:44,881 --> 00:04:46,880
Deep-fried Coca-Cola.

93
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:53,880
Mmm. That is good.

94
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:55,880
While these indulgences may only emerge

95
00:04:55,881 --> 00:04:57,880
from a deep fryer at fair time,

96
00:04:57,881 --> 00:04:59,880
the bustling griddle at Mickey's Diner

97
00:04:59,881 --> 00:05:01,880
in St. Paul, Minnesota,

98
00:05:01,881 --> 00:05:04,880
fries up comfort food 24 hours a day

99
00:05:04,881 --> 00:05:09,880
Fries up comfort food 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

100
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,560
And they've been doing it since 1939.

101
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:19,620
It's places like this where you'll find

102
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:22,100
some of the most experienced cooks in the world,

103
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:24,000
short order cooks.

104
00:05:24,001 --> 00:05:25,000
It's not for everybody.

105
00:05:25,001 --> 00:05:25,960
I mean, not all cooks.

106
00:05:25,961 --> 00:05:27,540
I mean, we've had a lot of cooks go through here.

107
00:05:27,541 --> 00:05:28,840
The more you concentrate on it,

108
00:05:28,841 --> 00:05:30,040
the more and more you fall behind.

109
00:05:30,041 --> 00:05:32,900
So you gotta keep your head clear.

110
00:05:32,901 --> 00:05:34,700
That's why I call it short order cooks,

111
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,800
because we only have to remember that much.

112
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:40,940
After more than 24 years as a short order cook,

113
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:43,640
Rick has learned to trust his senses in frying up everything

114
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:48,940
from buttery Denver omelets, burgers, and French fries,

115
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:52,560
to the diner's original pancake recipe.

116
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:57,060
One of the most popular items on the menu is hash browns.

117
00:05:57,160 --> 00:06:01,060
Rick fries up nearly 30 pounds of them a day,

118
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:04,060
That works out to over 170,000 pounds of hash browns

119
00:06:04,061 --> 00:06:06,060
in his 24 years.

120
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:10,060
We use real potatoes. That's the big thing.

121
00:06:10,061 --> 00:06:12,060
And we actually use lard.

122
00:06:12,061 --> 00:06:17,060
A lot of places use vegetable oil or some sort of pan fry oil

123
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:19,060
that's flavored with butter.

124
00:06:19,061 --> 00:06:22,060
Anything with fat in it tastes good. I don't care what it is.

125
00:06:22,061 --> 00:06:25,060
Talk to any chef.

126
00:06:25,061 --> 00:06:28,060
Fat is the key.

127
00:06:28,061 --> 00:06:30,860
While animal fat may add taste to your meal,

128
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:33,700
it's also a significant source of saturated fat

129
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:36,000
and cholesterol.

130
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:39,400
The result is a shrinking role in the frying universe.

131
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:41,900
In this country, we used animal fats,

132
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,200
then we used vegetable shortening.

133
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:48,620
Today, we use liquid oils like peanut oil, soybean oil,

134
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:50,220
corn, canola oils.

135
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:53,420
They have no cholesterol, unlike the animal fats,

136
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:56,460
like lard and tallow.

137
00:06:56,461 --> 00:07:02,460
Ventura Foods in Ontario, California knows their oil.

138
00:07:02,461 --> 00:07:06,460
They bottle over 100 different labels, including Luana, one of

139
00:07:06,461 --> 00:07:09,460
the oldest cooking oil brands in the nation.

140
00:07:09,461 --> 00:07:13,460
They produce a wide variety of cooking oils, including soybean.

141
00:07:13,461 --> 00:07:16,460
I think there's something like 12 billion pounds of soybean

142
00:07:16,461 --> 00:07:19,460
oil consumed in the United States every year.

143
00:07:19,461 --> 00:07:22,460
Bulk soybean and other oils arrive by the rail car at

144
00:07:22,461 --> 00:07:25,460
Ventura Foods from refineries across the country.

145
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:30,460
One of the tankers holds 185,000 pounds of oil,

146
00:07:30,461 --> 00:07:35,460
and we will use 10 of those a day in the factory.

147
00:07:35,461 --> 00:07:38,460
The oil is pumped in, filtered,

148
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:41,460
and stored in large silos until it's time for it to be bottled.

149
00:07:41,461 --> 00:07:43,460
Going into the bottle,

150
00:07:43,461 --> 00:07:47,460
we pump a small amount of nitrogen into the bottle

151
00:07:47,461 --> 00:07:49,460
to keep the oil fresh.

152
00:07:49,461 --> 00:07:51,460
The nitrogen replaces oxygen,

153
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:53,460
which keeps the oil from going rancid

154
00:07:53,461 --> 00:07:57,700
going rancid as it's stored over time.

155
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:59,900
Rancidity occurs as oil breaks down

156
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,660
and produces off flavors.

157
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:05,100
Because oil hinders bacterial growth,

158
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,700
rancid oil is relatively safe to consume,

159
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:10,320
just not as appetizing.

160
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:13,020
I've read of archaeologists finding anointing oil

161
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,420
in Israel that was 2,000 years old,

162
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:17,420
stuck their finger in it, tasted it.

163
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:20,020
Won't hurt you.

164
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:22,320
In the early 1900s, the food industry

165
00:08:22,321 --> 00:08:27,320
another way to make vegetable oil more resistant to rancidity.

166
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:31,060
It created vegetable shortening in a process called partial

167
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:35,660
hydrogenation. It was more stable, longer lasting,

168
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:39,480
and particularly appealing for commercial deep frying, requiring

169
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:43,880
a lot of oil every day. The downside?

170
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:47,420
The process also created trans fats, which increased consumers'

171
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:51,380
risk of heart disease. As people evolved to a healthier

172
00:08:51,381 --> 00:08:55,720
healthier cooking, they like to use the liquid oils.

173
00:08:55,721 --> 00:08:58,180
Ventura Foods even has a full-time chef

174
00:08:58,181 --> 00:09:01,020
to test and demonstrate their different oils.

175
00:09:01,021 --> 00:09:03,520
Now, how important is oil to the restaurant industry?

176
00:09:03,521 --> 00:09:06,020
Now, it's very important, considering that the majority

177
00:09:06,021 --> 00:09:08,940
of the foods that you order is fried.

178
00:09:08,941 --> 00:09:10,640
When it comes to frying, it's the oil

179
00:09:10,641 --> 00:09:14,200
that's responsible for transferring heat to your food.

180
00:09:14,201 --> 00:09:16,400
It's like boiling an egg in water,

181
00:09:16,401 --> 00:09:20,740
but at a much higher temperature and with more flavor.

182
00:09:20,741 --> 00:09:22,940
For frying, one of the most important characteristics

183
00:09:22,941 --> 00:09:26,440
of any oil is its smoke point, the temperature at which smoke

184
00:09:26,441 --> 00:09:29,440
starts coming off the oil.

185
00:09:29,441 --> 00:09:33,620
That's a good sign of deterioration and also

186
00:09:33,621 --> 00:09:35,320
olive flavor coming off from your oil.

187
00:09:35,321 --> 00:09:37,420
The smoke point's very important because you're frying

188
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:38,520
at such a high temperature.

189
00:09:38,521 --> 00:09:43,220
You're talking about up to 400 degrees.

190
00:09:43,221 --> 00:09:47,060
There's different smoke points for different types of oils.

191
00:09:47,061 --> 00:09:52,060
Extra virgin olive oil smokes around 375 degrees Fahrenheit,

192
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:57,400
while virgin olive oil holds out until 420 degrees.

193
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:01,940
Some lard may not smoke until 425 degrees.

194
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:07,120
And vegetable and nut oils range from 400 to 450 degrees.

195
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:08,520
If you don't turn down your temperature,

196
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:12,160
basically what the next point is is called the flash point.

197
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:15,660
Flash point is the point at which the oil will ignite

198
00:10:15,661 --> 00:10:18,500
and can bust.

199
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,300
Once you select your oil of choice,

200
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,400
it's time to turn on the flame and get frying.

201
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:24,900
There's different categories in frying.

202
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
There's sauteing.

203
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:29,100
There's searing.

204
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:32,620
There's also stir fry, which involves high heat.

205
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:33,920
And definitely there's deep frying,

206
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:36,480
which everyone's familiar with.

207
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:38,220
Stir frying cooks at temperatures

208
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:42,920
up to 600 degrees Fahrenheit for a short period of time.

209
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:45,660
At that temperature, the oil is just below flashpoint.

210
00:10:45,661 --> 00:10:49,660
It will likely smoke, but with only a small amount of oil,

211
00:10:49,661 --> 00:10:52,660
all flavors are not an issue.

212
00:10:52,661 --> 00:10:55,660
Sautéing involves a slightly lower temperature

213
00:10:55,661 --> 00:10:58,660
and generally cooks the food more thoroughly and caramelizes,

214
00:10:58,661 --> 00:11:00,660
or cooks the food's natural sugar,

215
00:11:00,661 --> 00:11:03,660
until it browns and creates a nutty flavor.

216
00:11:03,661 --> 00:11:06,660
You see a lot of smoke on here?

217
00:11:06,661 --> 00:11:07,660
That's just a lot of the water.

218
00:11:07,661 --> 00:11:09,660
So it's steaming, but basically, you know,

219
00:11:09,661 --> 00:11:11,660
you're caramelizing as well.

220
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:13,660
Talking about extreme temperatures here,

221
00:11:13,661 --> 00:11:16,160
here, about 400 degrees.

222
00:11:16,161 --> 00:11:19,660
Unlike sautéing, searing is only done with meat

223
00:11:19,661 --> 00:11:21,600
and only browns the outside.

224
00:11:21,601 --> 00:11:23,740
Everything you do on the outside is creating more flavor

225
00:11:23,741 --> 00:11:25,040
for the meat.

226
00:11:25,041 --> 00:11:27,700
And when moist meat hits hot oil, something else

227
00:11:27,701 --> 00:11:30,140
occurs in the pan.

228
00:11:30,141 --> 00:11:32,980
Once it's really wet and you drop it in the hot oil,

229
00:11:32,981 --> 00:11:36,140
you can get a lot of water jumping out and possibly

230
00:11:36,141 --> 00:11:37,720
hurt you.

231
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:39,620
The sizzling hot oil causes the water

232
00:11:39,621 --> 00:11:41,820
to boil out rapidly.

233
00:11:41,821 --> 00:11:47,460
As it does, some water shoots out, taking hot oil with it.

234
00:11:47,461 --> 00:11:49,760
For any type of frying, especially searing,

235
00:11:49,761 --> 00:11:52,160
it's important to fully heat the oil before placing

236
00:11:52,161 --> 00:11:54,700
the food in the pan.

237
00:11:54,701 --> 00:11:58,260
It sears the outside and creates

238
00:11:58,261 --> 00:12:01,180
kind of a barrier against the pan.

239
00:12:01,181 --> 00:12:05,520
And that creates a natural nonstick effect to your food.

240
00:12:05,521 --> 00:12:07,920
Well, I think what makes it just really good

241
00:12:07,921 --> 00:12:11,440
is all the brown bits of flavor that you can get

242
00:12:11,441 --> 00:12:13,240
pan searing your steak.

243
00:12:13,241 --> 00:12:16,020
I think without that, it would just be another plain piece

244
00:12:16,021 --> 00:12:16,820
of meat.

245
00:12:16,821 --> 00:12:18,980
Mm-hmm.

246
00:12:18,981 --> 00:12:21,020
But what happens when the meat being cooked

247
00:12:21,021 --> 00:12:23,820
is a well-seasoned whole turkey?

248
00:12:23,821 --> 00:12:26,160
Some backyard chefs go beyond the barbecue

249
00:12:26,161 --> 00:12:27,860
to the deep fryer.

250
00:12:27,861 --> 00:12:29,020
This is a guy thing.

251
00:12:29,021 --> 00:12:31,860
This is hanging outdoors and kind of playing with danger,

252
00:12:31,861 --> 00:12:37,060
fire, gloves, you know, grappling hook.

253
00:12:37,061 --> 00:12:39,100
While deep frying a turkey has been a part

254
00:12:39,101 --> 00:12:41,500
of Cajun cooking for nearly a century.

255
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:43,340
It's only recently spread to backyards

256
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:44,800
across the United States.

257
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:51,140
Whether you're cooking with propane or electricity,

258
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:55,340
the principles of deep frying turkey remain the same.

259
00:12:55,440 --> 00:13:01,460
Keep the skin on and fry in oil above 350 degrees Fahrenheit.

260
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:04,400
Both help keep in the moisture, keep out oil,

261
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:09,100
and cook the meat almost 75% faster than an oven.

262
00:13:09,101 --> 00:13:13,100
Inside the fryer, the hot oil envelops the turkey,

263
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:16,000
cooking the meat and searing the skin.

264
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:18,440
Meanwhile, the heat also causes the meat

265
00:13:18,540 --> 00:13:20,640
to release moisture.

266
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:22,980
This steam and the skin help keep oil

267
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:24,620
from entering the turkey.

268
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:26,620
If you don't make it hot enough,

269
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:28,120
you'll have greasy turkey.

270
00:13:28,220 --> 00:13:30,580
For a six-ounce portion of deep-fried turkey

271
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:32,760
versus oven-roasted, we're talking about a half-gram

272
00:13:32,860 --> 00:13:35,520
difference of fat from one over the other.

273
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:39,100
I mean, that's just the epitome of fast food.

274
00:13:39,101 --> 00:13:41,140
It's a deep-fried turkey.

275
00:13:41,141 --> 00:13:43,240
Can't get any better.

276
00:13:43,241 --> 00:13:46,400
Deep-frying this gobbler is impressive.

277
00:13:46,401 --> 00:13:49,540
But if you're frying chicken for 7,000,

278
00:13:49,541 --> 00:13:52,340
you might just need the world's largest pan

279
00:13:52,341 --> 00:13:57,360
and a couple of hundred gallons of oil.

280
00:13:57,361 --> 00:14:01,560
Soybean oil accounts for over 70% of all oils or fats

281
00:14:01,561 --> 00:14:04,060
consumed in the United States.

282
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:14,100
The smell, the sizzle of the oil, and of course the taste.

283
00:14:14,101 --> 00:14:19,100
Nothing seems to zap our senses quite like fried foods.

284
00:14:19,101 --> 00:14:22,100
And for some of us, there's no better example

285
00:14:22,101 --> 00:14:25,100
than fried chicken.

286
00:14:25,101 --> 00:14:29,100
Every year, over 250,000 people flock to the small town

287
00:14:29,101 --> 00:14:34,100
of London, Kentucky to celebrate the World Chicken Festival.

288
00:14:34,101 --> 00:14:36,100
Hey.

289
00:14:36,101 --> 00:14:40,100
Over three days, visitors will devour roughly 7,000 fried

290
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:41,100
chicken dinners.

291
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:43,400
There you are.

292
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:46,440
All originating from this, the world's largest

293
00:14:46,540 --> 00:14:48,040
stainless steel skillet.

294
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:50,080
The skillet itself is 10 and 1 1

295
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:51,080
feet across.

296
00:14:51,180 --> 00:14:52,860
It's six inches deep.

297
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:56,160
There's eight burners that fire this.

298
00:14:56,260 --> 00:15:00,460
We use enough natural gas for this skillet in a three-day

299
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:04,900
period to heat a normal house for a year.

300
00:15:04,901 --> 00:15:08,700
This frying marble can cook up an astonishing 320 chicken

301
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:11,700
quarters at a time.

302
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:14,840
To manage this behemoth, a team lightly coats the chickens

303
00:15:14,940 --> 00:15:18,740
in a flour and spice rub and monitors them as they fry in

304
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:23,460
300 gallons of peanut oil for roughly 25 minutes.

305
00:15:23,560 --> 00:15:25,860
When it floats to the top, that means it's getting ready.

306
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:28,160
I mean, you won't say it's getting done.

307
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:31,100
You try to be pretty gentle with them and roll them around.

308
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:33,700
You just pull them in where you can reach them out.

309
00:15:33,701 --> 00:15:37,200
Yeah, it's good done.

310
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:39,300
Once fried, the chicken has one more stop

311
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,640
before it makes it to a plate.

312
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,980
I think the biggest secret is getting it out here

313
00:15:44,080 --> 00:15:46,740
and letting it sit about 15 minutes in them coolers.

314
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:50,720
This steams the chicken and makes it moister.

315
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:54,420
A good pan may be frying's best friend.

316
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:57,760
Over 5,000 years ago, ancient Mesopotamians

317
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:01,760
cooked with some of the first frying pans.

318
00:16:01,761 --> 00:16:04,700
While early pans were often made of copper, brass, or

319
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:07,900
bronze, it would be iron that would rule the frying world

320
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:09,200
by the 19th century.

321
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:15,000
Today, the small town of South Pittsburgh, Tennessee,

322
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:17,700
is home to the only foundry in the United States still

323
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:21,520
making the cast iron cookware we've depended on for over 200

324
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:23,220
years.

325
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:26,720
It was really America's original cookware, back before

326
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:30,360
aluminum and stainless steel, which dominate the cookware

327
00:16:30,361 --> 00:16:32,360
cookware market today.

328
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:36,940
Family owned and operated since 1896,

329
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:41,680
the Lodge Manufacturing Company pours, molds, and packages

330
00:16:41,681 --> 00:16:44,720
over 100 varieties of cast iron cookware,

331
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:49,320
including Dutch ovens, woks, and of course, frying pans.

332
00:16:49,860 --> 00:16:52,460
Depending on the product that's ordered that day,

333
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:57,440
we have two lines that can make between 400 and 1,600 pieces

334
00:16:57,441 --> 00:16:59,060
of cast iron an hour.

335
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:04,760
All those pieces start here with three primary ingredients,

336
00:17:04,860 --> 00:17:11,200
pig iron, recycled cast iron cookware, and stamped steel.

337
00:17:11,300 --> 00:17:12,800
The foundry melts them all together

338
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:14,900
to achieve a proper cast iron chemistry.

339
00:17:19,420 --> 00:17:23,620
A container transfers this blend to the molding area.

340
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:26,020
Here, a machine presses river sand

341
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:29,820
into two-sided molds, which are filled with a molten cast iron.

342
00:17:29,821 --> 00:17:33,520
iron to form the ideal pan.

343
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:38,320
The pans will cool and shake off sand to be recycled.

344
00:17:38,420 --> 00:17:40,660
The cookware goes through several buffing and cleaning

345
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:44,860
processes, including a rock polish to remove more sand

346
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,480
and rough edges.

347
00:17:47,580 --> 00:17:50,380
For centuries, when someone bought a new cast iron pan,

348
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:52,280
it looked like this.

349
00:17:52,380 --> 00:17:54,980
It wasn't seasoned.

350
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:58,980
Seasoning creates a nonstick effect on cast iron cookware.

351
00:17:58,981 --> 00:18:01,480
Recently, Lodge began pre-seasoning all cookware

352
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:04,920
for their customers.

353
00:18:05,020 --> 00:18:07,280
They begin by spraying soy-based vegetable oil

354
00:18:07,380 --> 00:18:11,120
across the entire piece.

355
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:14,120
This oil coats the pan's surface.

356
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:17,440
An industrial oven heats the oil and pan to over 400 degrees

357
00:18:17,540 --> 00:18:20,740
Fahrenheit for roughly 20 minutes,

358
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:23,140
causing all the elements of the oil to burn off,

359
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:26,240
except for the carbon.

360
00:18:26,340 --> 00:18:28,540
These carbon deposits seal the surface,

361
00:18:28,541 --> 00:18:30,980
including any microscopic pores,

362
00:18:31,080 --> 00:18:33,580
keeping food from sticking to the pan.

363
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:36,680
It's the carbon deposit, which is really your seasoning.

364
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:39,480
And this old skillet that's 60 years old

365
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:43,080
is as smooth as a baby's bottom.

366
00:18:43,180 --> 00:18:47,800
With proper care, it could easily last for centuries.

367
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:51,600
Regularly cooking with cast iron re-seasons the surface.

368
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:53,040
But there's another benefit.

369
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:54,900
It's been substantiated that cast iron

370
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,140
imparts dietary iron into the food,

371
00:18:57,141 --> 00:19:01,040
which is absorbed by the body, so it's healthy to cook in.

372
00:19:01,041 --> 00:19:03,640
Having a durable nonstick surface that can maintain

373
00:19:03,641 --> 00:19:06,740
an even heat of 325 degrees Fahrenheit

374
00:19:06,741 --> 00:19:11,240
is especially helpful when pan-frying southern fried chicken.

375
00:19:11,241 --> 00:19:17,340
This is the difference between southern frying and sautéing.

376
00:19:17,341 --> 00:19:20,540
In sautéing, you move the food around.

377
00:19:20,541 --> 00:19:24,940
In southern frying or pan-frying, you leave it alone.

378
00:19:24,941 --> 00:19:29,940
You're gonna let this pan do what this pan is meant to do,

379
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:33,540
which is form the crust on the chicken.

380
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:36,980
And this is also the difference between deep frying.

381
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:40,280
With deep frying, the food is completely immersed.

382
00:19:41,900 --> 00:19:44,600
One of the secrets to having really good

383
00:19:44,700 --> 00:19:47,000
southern fried chicken is that it's crispy.

384
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:49,240
And if you drain it on paper towels,

385
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:51,200
you're increasing sogginess.

386
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:53,500
Take your wire baking rack

387
00:19:53,501 --> 00:19:55,460
and put your fried chicken on that,

388
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:56,940
then that keeps it crispy.

389
00:19:57,680 --> 00:19:59,960
The ultimate in southern fried chicken.

390
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:02,060
Crunchy on the outside,

391
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:05,700
completely done and moist on the inside,

392
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:07,800
and served piping hot.

393
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:11,700
Mmm.

394
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:14,220
Perfect.

395
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:18,520
Since the 1930s,

396
00:20:18,521 --> 00:20:21,020
the Sanders Cafe in Corbin, Kentucky

397
00:20:21,021 --> 00:20:23,380
has fried up what Colonel Harlan Sanders

398
00:20:23,381 --> 00:20:25,780
also considered the perfect fried chicken,

399
00:20:25,880 --> 00:20:30,180
the special blend of 11 herbs and spices.

400
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:33,420
This top secret recipe has remained unchanged

401
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:35,720
and served as an extremely successful advertising

402
00:20:35,820 --> 00:20:38,720
campaign for KFC.

403
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:42,540
To the frying itself, the colonel made a few adjustments.

404
00:20:42,640 --> 00:20:46,140
He started out fried in skillets.

405
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:48,980
Then he went to the pressure cooker,

406
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:53,380
cooked about 18 pieces of chicken this for nine minutes.

407
00:20:53,381 --> 00:20:55,780
In fact, the kernel was one of the first to fry chicken

408
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:58,180
under pressure.

409
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:02,380
This method led to the creation of the pressure fryer.

410
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:04,620
It works similarly to a turkey fryer,

411
00:21:04,720 --> 00:21:09,620
but utilizes pressure to speed up the cooking process.

412
00:21:09,720 --> 00:21:11,840
Within the sealed cooker, the chicken's moisture

413
00:21:11,940 --> 00:21:16,540
begins to evaporate, in turn building up pressure.

414
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:18,440
This causes the water's boiling point

415
00:21:18,540 --> 00:21:21,280
to rise to 245 degrees Fahrenheit,

416
00:21:21,281 --> 00:21:25,780
than 212 degrees at atmospheric pressure.

417
00:21:25,880 --> 00:21:28,280
This temperature difference reduces cooking time

418
00:21:28,380 --> 00:21:30,080
and retains more moisture in the chicken.

419
00:21:33,220 --> 00:21:35,020
As pressure fryers brew in size,

420
00:21:35,120 --> 00:21:38,240
frying up to 64 pieces in just 12 minutes,

421
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:40,240
Kentucky Fried Chicken also expanded.

422
00:21:44,140 --> 00:21:47,240
Today, there are over 11,000 restaurants in 80 countries

423
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:50,340
around the world, producing more than a billion chicken

424
00:21:50,341 --> 00:21:51,340
every year.

425
00:21:53,580 --> 00:21:56,640
Like many fast food chains, in 2007,

426
00:21:56,641 --> 00:21:59,080
it also switched from frying with trans fats

427
00:21:59,081 --> 00:22:00,580
to healthier vegetable oil.

428
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:04,880
Stringer looking good.

429
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:10,800
With the worldwide popularity of fast food restaurants

430
00:22:10,801 --> 00:22:12,800
came the global spread of fryers.

431
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:17,700
So where do they all come from?

432
00:22:17,701 --> 00:22:19,700
Most people probably don't realize it.

433
00:22:19,701 --> 00:22:23,400
but when they're going to restaurants here or around the world,

434
00:22:23,401 --> 00:22:26,740
they're probably getting french fries or fried chicken

435
00:22:26,741 --> 00:22:31,740
or that fish sandwich that was fried in a Henny Penny fryer.

436
00:22:31,741 --> 00:22:33,740
Based out of Eaton, Ohio,

437
00:22:33,741 --> 00:22:39,160
Henny Penny built the first commercial pressure fryer in 1957.

438
00:22:39,161 --> 00:22:42,360
Today, they manufacture and ship thousands of pressure fryers

439
00:22:42,361 --> 00:22:47,360
and open fryers to countries all over the world every year.

440
00:22:47,361 --> 00:22:51,360
Open fryers have changed quite a bit from just an open pot

441
00:22:51,460 --> 00:22:55,360
that people would dump their product into and pull it out

442
00:22:55,460 --> 00:22:57,000
when a timer would ding.

443
00:22:57,100 --> 00:23:00,000
Now everything is programmed within that fryer

444
00:23:00,100 --> 00:23:05,020
to where an operator can just push a button and select fries.

445
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:07,020
They don't have to think about it.

446
00:23:07,120 --> 00:23:11,020
It even tells them when it's time to filter the oil.

447
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:13,660
Filtering oil is a necessity for many restaurants

448
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:16,660
requiring a lot of cooking oil for their fryers.

449
00:23:16,661 --> 00:23:19,900
As bits of food remain in the hot oil,

450
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,360
they continue to release moisture and oxygen,

451
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:24,600
causing the oil to go rancid faster.

452
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,200
As fryers have evolved, so have their filtering systems.

453
00:23:30,300 --> 00:23:32,600
In normal frying operations,

454
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:34,720
filtering happens once or twice a day,

455
00:23:34,820 --> 00:23:38,420
and operators typically see 7 to 10 days of oil life.

456
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:40,520
By using this automated filtering process,

457
00:23:40,620 --> 00:23:42,960
they can filter as many as eight times a day,

458
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:45,960
and they might see as much as three weeks of oil life.

459
00:23:45,961 --> 00:23:50,960
That means buying a lot less oil to produce a lot of fried food.

460
00:23:50,961 --> 00:23:55,960
Penny Penny's innovations also apply to their pressure fryers,

461
00:23:55,961 --> 00:23:58,960
which remain their most popular products.

462
00:23:58,961 --> 00:24:03,960
The purpose of the fryers is to make frying as easy as possible.

463
00:24:03,961 --> 00:24:07,960
We take the pan of chicken and we place it right here.

464
00:24:07,961 --> 00:24:10,960
So now to cook the chicken, we take the big pieces first

465
00:24:10,961 --> 00:24:13,960
and we place them in. This is called dropping.

466
00:24:13,961 --> 00:24:17,960
I'm going from the front to the back.

467
00:24:17,961 --> 00:24:19,960
So touch, release.

468
00:24:19,961 --> 00:24:21,960
This is the universal thing.

469
00:24:21,961 --> 00:24:25,960
Touch and start.

470
00:24:25,961 --> 00:24:27,960
And that's how we fry chicken.

471
00:24:27,961 --> 00:24:29,960
But there's a world of frying out there,

472
00:24:29,961 --> 00:24:33,960
so get ready to walk on the wild side.

473
00:24:33,961 --> 00:24:35,960
I'm gonna rock and roll right now.

474
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:41,960
In 1987, Kentucky Fried Chicken became the first American

475
00:24:41,961 --> 00:24:44,600
American fast food chain in China.

476
00:24:44,601 --> 00:24:48,960
By 2008, China had over 1,800 KFCs.

477
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:58,960
Almost every culture around the world

478
00:24:58,961 --> 00:25:01,960
loves its fried food.

479
00:25:01,961 --> 00:25:06,960
The Chinese fry up kong yu bing, spring rolls, and wontons,

480
00:25:06,961 --> 00:25:10,960
while Armenians prefer coatlets, cuckoo, and falafels.

481
00:25:10,961 --> 00:25:14,640
and falafels, and don't forget Indian samosas,

482
00:25:14,641 --> 00:25:19,360
French beignets, and Mexican taquitos.

483
00:25:19,361 --> 00:25:21,840
At British pubs like the Cat and Fiddle,

484
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:24,480
fish and chips are standard fare,

485
00:25:24,481 --> 00:25:28,220
traditionally whitefish and fries.

486
00:25:28,221 --> 00:25:33,180
The busy kitchen keeps head chef John Sowell's on his toes.

487
00:25:33,181 --> 00:25:35,420
We made ours the way I made it in England,

488
00:25:35,421 --> 00:25:40,420
with the beer, the baking powder, and pasty flour.

489
00:25:42,060 --> 00:25:43,740
After John dips the fish,

490
00:25:43,741 --> 00:25:47,780
he tosses it in 375 degree oil, along with the chips.

491
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:52,420
After a few minutes, the golden brown fish and chips

492
00:25:52,421 --> 00:25:53,520
are ready for tasting.

493
00:25:56,920 --> 00:25:58,620
It's delicious.

494
00:25:58,621 --> 00:26:01,900
The fish is moist, you see, so creamy,

495
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:04,220
and the batter is very crispy.

496
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:07,380
Too fishy for your taste?

497
00:26:07,381 --> 00:26:09,460
How about a Scotch egg?

498
00:26:09,461 --> 00:26:11,960
It's a boiled egg wrapped in sausage meat.

499
00:26:11,961 --> 00:26:13,260
We make our own here.

500
00:26:13,261 --> 00:26:15,260
And flour, egg, and bread crumbed.

501
00:26:15,261 --> 00:26:17,260
And fry it till it's cooked.

502
00:26:17,261 --> 00:26:20,100
It's a nice shape, crumbed and deep fat fried.

503
00:26:20,101 --> 00:26:22,640
And in England, it's cooled and served on a salad.

504
00:26:22,641 --> 00:26:26,140
But here, they've always served them warm.

505
00:26:26,141 --> 00:26:27,980
Nearly halfway across the world,

506
00:26:27,981 --> 00:26:31,680
another island nation also loves its deep frying.

507
00:26:31,681 --> 00:26:34,780
In Japan, tempura, lightly battered fish

508
00:26:34,781 --> 00:26:39,780
or vegetables has been a popular snack for several centuries.

509
00:26:39,781 --> 00:26:42,780
Often served alongside sushi, it's a hot item on the menu

510
00:26:42,781 --> 00:26:44,780
at Japanese restaurants like sakura.

511
00:26:48,780 --> 00:26:51,780
The key to any tempura is a batter that won't lay a thick coat

512
00:26:51,781 --> 00:26:53,780
on your food.

513
00:26:53,781 --> 00:27:00,780
The thinner batter requires less frying time,

514
00:27:00,781 --> 00:27:03,780
so tempura normally involves vegetables or small portions

515
00:27:03,781 --> 00:27:05,780
which can cook quickly.

516
00:27:05,781 --> 00:27:11,780
Then, like this, a little bit.

