615
00:32:36,640 --> 00:32:40,640
We run about 280,000 pounds a week of tortilla chips

616
00:32:40,740 --> 00:32:46,080
and about 50,000 pounds a week on taco shells.

617
00:32:46,081 --> 00:32:49,280
Tortilla chips, known as totopos in Mexico,

618
00:32:49,380 --> 00:32:51,580
started as a way to preserve day-old tortillas.

619
00:32:58,420 --> 00:33:00,620
Anita's starts with whole kernels of corn.

620
00:33:02,740 --> 00:33:05,240
On the day of our taping, it happened to be blue corn.

621
00:33:06,840 --> 00:33:09,540
What we do is we clean the corn, we cook the corn,

622
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:14,740
we steep the corn, grind it, and then we make our masa,

623
00:33:14,840 --> 00:33:16,080
or dough.

624
00:33:16,081 --> 00:33:22,120
Workers keep an eye on the thick masa as a machine feeds it into a cookie cutter type

625
00:33:22,121 --> 00:33:30,403
of apparatus. This rolls and cuts the masa into the desired chip shape.

626
00:33:30,404 --> 00:33:31,920
The masa then

627
00:33:31,921 --> 00:33:37,920
travels through an enormous 375 degree Fahrenheit baking oven for roughly two minutes.

628
00:33:37,921 --> 00:33:44,920
Basically, you're turning a cornmeal into a tortilla.

629
00:33:44,921 --> 00:33:47,920
While it's not round like your conventional tortilla,

630
00:33:47,921 --> 00:33:50,920
it's still the same exact concept, same moisture,

631
00:33:50,921 --> 00:33:54,920
same qualities as a tortilla, just a shape.

632
00:33:54,921 --> 00:33:56,920
The soon-to-be tortilla chips

633
00:33:56,921 --> 00:34:01,920
take a long, carefully-timed ride to the fryer to cool off.

634
00:34:01,921 --> 00:34:05,920
As they do, they also release moisture.

635
00:34:05,921 --> 00:34:08,920
The less moisture the product has going into the fryer,

636
00:34:09,020 --> 00:34:12,320
the less oil and fat it will absorb.

637
00:34:12,420 --> 00:34:15,160
The chips going into the fryer have approximately

638
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:18,160
a 25% moisture.

639
00:34:18,260 --> 00:34:22,300
Coming out of the fryer, you're down about 1.5%.

640
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:26,140
We have to find that balance between not too much oil

641
00:34:26,240 --> 00:34:28,380
but enough that will give us the product

642
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:30,380
that we want at the end.

643
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:33,540
So what would they have without the baking process?

644
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:35,920
Corn chips.

645
00:34:35,921 --> 00:34:37,860
You just fry it directly.

646
00:34:37,861 --> 00:34:40,760
Now, that means that you're going to have much more oil

647
00:34:40,761 --> 00:34:44,520
in a corn chip than you have in a tortilla chip.

648
00:34:44,521 --> 00:34:48,460
About 25% more oil by weight.

649
00:34:48,461 --> 00:34:51,160
For tortilla chips, the delicious transformation

650
00:34:51,161 --> 00:34:54,780
from masa to chip happens in here, the continuous fryer.

651
00:34:57,680 --> 00:35:01,580
Inside, 2,500 pounds of hot sunflower oil

652
00:35:01,581 --> 00:35:04,120
cycle through at any given time in order

653
00:35:04,121 --> 00:35:06,120
in order to keep frying chips flowing.

654
00:35:08,120 --> 00:35:10,620
Oil is very important for our tortilla chips

655
00:35:10,621 --> 00:35:15,120
because not only does it give you that mouthful flavor and taste,

656
00:35:15,121 --> 00:35:19,120
it also protects it from the outside environment,

657
00:35:19,121 --> 00:35:23,120
from the air coming in, from the moisture gathering onto it.

658
00:35:23,121 --> 00:35:27,120
It also gives you the extended shelf life as well.

659
00:35:28,120 --> 00:35:30,620
Basically, at this point, the tortilla has now been fried

660
00:35:30,621 --> 00:35:33,120
and it's turned into a finished product, which is a chip.

661
00:35:33,121 --> 00:35:38,120
It went through a temperature of 370 degrees, roughly for about 45 seconds.

662
00:35:38,121 --> 00:35:43,300
All that's left now is to cool it off, put some salt on it, put it in the bag and send

663
00:35:43,301 --> 00:35:45,300
it off to the customer.

664
00:35:45,301 --> 00:35:50,300
Anita's is one of a growing number of food processors that fries with healthier oils.

665
00:35:50,301 --> 00:36:00,180
We fry in high oleic sunflower oil, and it's an oil that is high in mono and polysaturated

666
00:36:00,181 --> 00:36:02,780
but it's low on the saturated fat.

667
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:06,480
It's also very high in vitamin E.

668
00:36:06,580 --> 00:36:08,820
They also use this oil to fry the majority

669
00:36:08,920 --> 00:36:10,920
of their taco shells.

670
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:14,820
Well, a taco shell starts its life like a tortilla chip.

671
00:36:14,920 --> 00:36:19,240
But instead of stamping out small triangles or small strips,

672
00:36:19,340 --> 00:36:23,740
we will stamp out a 5 inch or 6 inch diameter shell.

673
00:36:27,880 --> 00:36:30,180
When it goes into the fryer, it is

674
00:36:30,181 --> 00:36:33,940
is put together via mold to form the shape of the taco shell.

675
00:36:39,300 --> 00:36:44,640
It will come out, be packaged, and put into boxes

676
00:36:44,641 --> 00:36:45,680
to go out to the customer.

677
00:36:48,740 --> 00:36:51,060
Both taco shells and fried tortilla chips

678
00:36:51,061 --> 00:36:54,400
compete with their baked counterparts.

679
00:36:54,401 --> 00:36:56,860
Fried tortilla chips has probably somewhere

680
00:36:56,861 --> 00:37:00,700
between six to seven grams of fat per one ounce serving.

681
00:37:00,701 --> 00:37:02,840
On the other hand, a baked tortilla chip

682
00:37:02,841 --> 00:37:05,760
that has been twice baked will only give you

683
00:37:05,761 --> 00:37:08,500
about three grams of fat per one ounce serving.

684
00:37:09,700 --> 00:37:12,880
Quite frankly, the reason the fried chips are so popular

685
00:37:12,881 --> 00:37:15,600
is because it's fried, it's very flavorful.

686
00:37:15,601 --> 00:37:19,180
And I dare anyone to eat one tortilla chip.

